From: mscharged-port
Subject: Render area of multisampled EFB passes
Upstream-base: d664382f57002fbff143911c81b06b7e7a4b3ab4
Classification: host performance. Apply after 0123.

With multisampling every EFB pass resolves the whole EFB when it ends, and
every EFB copy ends a pass: a Vice match frame resolves the whole EFB 43
times, 32 of them for the 64x64 crowd impostors the game draws in its top
left corner (+9 ms of GPU time per frame at 4x on an Intel UHD 620).

Each pass now records the bounding box of its draws' scissor rectangles (GX
draws: the scissor in effect; partial clears: their rectangle; any other
draw: the whole EFB). A multisampled EFB pass without clears names that box
as its render area (Dawn RenderPassRenderAreaRect, Vulkan), and Vulkan
loads, stores and resolves only inside it. Every pass resolves every pixel
it can change, so the resolved EFB that copies read stays the resolve of the
whole EFB. A pass without draws or clears is not begun at all. Scissors no
draw uses (the state a pass starts with) shrink into the area, which Dawn
requires. Other backends keep the whole EFB.

Same draws, same resolve of each pixel: the pixels do not change.

